;; A WORKED EXAMPLE shipped with the seed — not a configuration. Copy the one
;; matching your seat (`--seat <name>`, default claude) to <name>-build/profile.edn; edit it there.
;;
;; SEAT :agy-ide — the Antigravity IDE's CLI. Its sibling is claude.edn, and the
;; pair is instructive: they are near mirror images. Here Google writes and
;; Anthropic reviews; there Anthropic writes and Google reviews. Between them
;; both tool-call adapters are exercised, which is why there are two rather
;; than one.
;;
;; Model names are dated selections (decision log P0-3: "Specific models are
;; dated selections"). The SHAPE is the part meant to outlive them. The Tester
;; below was chosen by a bake-off during the KIT's development, whose records
;; are not in this repository; no run has ever used this seat, so nothing else
;; here is measured and the comments say so where it matters.
;;
;; Three things this file is careful about:
;;
;;   ONE SEAT. The interactive client is where the human sits. The dispatched
;;   roles are reached over HTTP by the harness and are not anyone's editor —
;;   so there is exactly one :seat, and it is a scalar so it cannot grow a
;;   second. :seat names a tool `bb doctor` knows how to probe.
;;
;;   THE CODER'S FAMILY FOLLOWS THE SEAT, by convention and not by rule. The
;;   seat here is a Google client, so the Coder is Gemini and Anthropic moves to
;;   the verifying side — the exact reverse of claude.edn. It is not checked,
;;   because OpenCode 2 and Pi are bring-your-own-model clients with no inherent
;;   family: the relation is not expressible for half the seats this kit
;;   supports.
;;
;;   VERIFIER ≠ CODER FAMILY. Method §05 and decision log P0-3, and this one IS
;;   checked. The Tester and the Reviewer both verify the Coder, so neither may
;;   share its family, nor each other's. `bb profile` is the only place that
;;   rule has ever been anything but prose.
;;
;;   NO KEYS, EVER. :key-env NAMES an environment variable. This file is
;;   committed; a field that could hold a secret would eventually be handed one.

{:seat :agy-ide

 :roles
 {;; CODER — gemini-3.8-flash at medium effort, over OpenRouter.
  ;;
  ;; PINNED REQUEST-SIDE: without :allow_fallbacks false, the provider you
  ;; logged yesterday is not the one you get today, and a different provider can
  ;; mean a different chat template.
  ;;
  ;; MEDIUM, NOT HIGH, AND THAT IS MEASURED. The original sketch put
  ;; :reasoning_effort "high" on every OpenRouter role, unexamined. On
  ;; gemini-3.8-flash as Reviewer it produced minutes of thinking and an
  ;; EMPTY final message; the identical dispatch at medium answered quickly,
  ;; for less, with real findings. High reasoning on a flash tier spends
  ;; the budget thinking and has nothing left to say with.
  :coder {:family :google
          :model "google/gemini-3.8-flash"
          :shape :openai
          :endpoint "https://openrouter.ai/api/v1"
          :key-env "OPENROUTER_API_KEY"
          :params {:reasoning_effort "medium"
                   :provider {:only ["google-vertex"] :allow_fallbacks false}}}

  ;; TESTER — gpt-5.6-sol at medium effort. Dated selection 2026-09-14.
  ;;
  ;; CHOOSE THE TESTER BY ITS TESTS, NOT ITS PRICE — see claude.edn. The same
  ;; Tester as that profile: the result does not depend on the seat, and openai
  ;; is neither Coder's family. The standard endpoint rather than flex, because
  ;; flex was rate-limited upstream on most requests when this was switched;
  ;; claude.edn has the reasoning, and why the "openai" pin is observed rather
  ;; than guaranteed to mean standard.
  :tester {:family :openai
           :model "openai/gpt-5.6-sol"
           :shape :openai
           :endpoint "https://openrouter.ai/api/v1"
           :key-env "OPENROUTER_API_KEY"
           :params {:reasoning_effort "medium"
                    :provider {:only ["openai"] :allow_fallbacks false}}}

  ;; REVIEWER — Claude Opus 5.5 at high effort, direct to Anthropic, which is
  ;; the :anthropic tool-call shape: this role and the spec reviewer are the
  ;; KIT's only worked example of that shape, and they stay direct for that
  ;; reason. No :provider pin: that endpoint does no routing, so there is
  ;; nothing to pin. No fallback model answers a refusal, and the cost is
  ;; computed from :pricing because the endpoint reports none.
  ;;
  ;; A DATED SELECTION OF 2026-10-05, BY ANALOGY, NOT MEASURED HERE. It was
  ;; Fable 5.1 (low, then high, then medium from 2026-09-18 by the person's
  ;; judgement). Two things moved it: Opus lists at well under half of Fable's
  ;; price (`bb models`), and the first real project found that review, not
  ;; code, is where a task's money goes - so the reader's price is the seat's
  ;; biggest lever; and Opus is the Blueprint reviewer in both shipped
  ;; profiles, where on that project it read a stage's Blueprint whole and
  ;; drew findings the Architect acted on. A Reviewer reads a diff against a
  ;; contract, the same kind of work. High, as claude.edn's reviewers run.
  ;; What is not measured is this seat as a whole: no run has used it, and no
  ;; Anthropic model has been dispatched as a Reviewer at all - the bake-off
  ;; that would test one could not, because every case's code was written by
  ;; an Anthropic Coder and §05 wants the verifier clear of the author's
  ;; family. The first agy-ide run tests it; `bb bake-off` is how to choose
  ;; from a measurement instead.
  ;;
  ;; :max_tokens 32,000, because thinking counts against that cap on this
  ;; shape. A Reviewer's findings are short, so the risk is not a long answer
  ;; but a budget spent thinking with nothing left to say it with — which this
  ;; kit has seen once, on a flash tier at high as Reviewer (see claude.edn).
  :reviewer {:family :anthropic
             :model "claude-opus-5-5"
             :shape :anthropic
             :endpoint "https://api.anthropic.com"
             :key-env "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"
             :params {:output_config {:effort "high"}
                      :max_tokens 32000}
             ;; USD per million tokens: base input, output, 5-minute and 1-hour
             ;; cache writes, cache reads. Input and output as `bb models` listed
             ;; them on 2026-10-05; the cache rates by Anthropic's multipliers
             ;; (1.25x and 2x the input for writes, 0.1x for reads).
             :pricing {:per-mtok {:in 4 :out 20
                                  :cache-write-5m 5 :cache-write-1h 8
                                  :cache-read 0.4}
                       :source "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing"
                       :as-of "2026-10-05"}}

  ;; PLAN REVIEWER — the spec reviewer's selection, block for block, as in
  ;; claude.edn and for the reason its comment gives: the plan review and the
  ;; spec review are the same kind of reading - cold, adversarial, one call -
  ;; and differ in the checklist sent, not in the reader. Its own role from
  ;; 2026-10-06 so that a project can set it apart; not the seat's family
  ;; (Google here), and `bb profile` checks that as it does the spec reviewer's.
  ;; One call per stage, in its plan step, by `bb plan-review`. NOT MEASURED
  ;; IN THIS SEAT, as nothing here is.
  :plan-reviewer {:family :anthropic
                  :model "claude-opus-5-5"
                  :shape :anthropic
                  :endpoint "https://api.anthropic.com"
                  :key-env "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"
                  :params {:output_config {:effort "high"}
                           :max_tokens 16000}
                  :pricing {:per-mtok {:in 4 :out 20
                                       :cache-write-5m 5 :cache-write-1h 8
                                       :cache-read 0.4}
                            :source "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing"
                            :as-of "2026-10-05"}}

  ;; SPEC REVIEWER — the Reviewer's family, for the Reviewer's reason: not the
  ;; seat's family (Google here), and the seat is where the Architect writes
  ;; the spec this role reads cold before `start`. The Reviewer's model too,
  ;; Opus 5.5 at high, the same dated selection of 2026-10-05 by analogy; it
  ;; was Fable 5.1 at low (2026-09-18).
  ;;
  ;; WHAT WAS MEASURED WAS FABLE'S EFFORT, NOT THIS. Fable reviewed the same
  ;; specs at both efforts on the claude seat: high cost nearly twice low per
  ;; call and hit its budget cap after twenty calls; low found the same four
  ;; gaps. That measurement is Fable's and does not carry to another model;
  ;; claude.edn's spec reviewer runs at high, and so does this one until the
  ;; first agy-ide run says otherwise. What is not measured is this seat as a
  ;; whole.
  :spec-reviewer {:family :anthropic
                  :model "claude-opus-5-5"
                  :shape :anthropic
                  :endpoint "https://api.anthropic.com"
                  :key-env "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"
                  :params {:output_config {:effort "high"}
                           :max_tokens 16000}
                  :pricing {:per-mtok {:in 4 :out 20
                                       :cache-write-5m 5 :cache-write-1h 8
                                       :cache-read 0.4}
                            :source "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing"
                            :as-of "2026-10-05"}}

  ;; BLUEPRINT REVIEWER — Claude Opus 5.5 at high effort, over OpenRouter
  ;; pinned to Anthropic's own serving: the same block as claude.edn's, for the
  ;; same measured reason (its comment has it). Here it is NOT the seat's
  ;; family — the seat is Google — so no exception is needed: this is the
  ;; arrangement §05 would ask for anyway. One call per Blueprint, before
  ;; sign-off, by `bb blueprint-review`; no tools, no cache, no :pricing.
  ;;
  ;; NOT MEASURED IN THIS SEAT. No run has used it; what was measured is the
  ;; model reading Blueprints written by the other seat's family.
  :blueprint-reviewer {:family :anthropic
                       :model "anthropic/claude-opus-5.5"
                       :shape :openai
                       :endpoint "https://openrouter.ai/api/v1"
                       :key-env "OPENROUTER_API_KEY"
                       :params {:reasoning_effort "high"
                                :max_tokens 16000
                                :provider {:only ["anthropic"] :allow_fallbacks false}}}

  ;; SECURITY REVIEWER — gpt-6-astra at high effort, over OpenRouter pinned to OpenAI. Dated
  ;; selection 2026-10-08, by two bake-offs on the KIT's planted-fault fixture (`bb security-bake-off`):
  ;; once per stage, at its end, it reads the merged application with five tools in a sandbox with
  ;; no network, and a finding counts only when a test of its own fails. It found six and five of
  ;; six planted faults, and on the careful branch its only failing tests were two real flaws
  ;; nobody had planted; the other candidate found three in every run and never answered inside
  ;; its round limit. Fable 5.1 declined the review (its safeguard, three runs) and was
  ;; not a candidate after.
  ;;
  ;; ONE SECURITY REVIEWER FOR NOW, the person's decision: a second from another family may be
  ;; tried later for diversity. It is a verifier - it reads what the Coder wrote to disagree
  ;; with it - so `bb profile` holds it to another family than the Coder's.
  ;;
  ;; WHAT IT COSTS: $0.46 to $1.50 a reading of the fixture, three to eight minutes of review;
  ;; a real stage is larger. Its reproduced findings go to triage (`bb security-route`), its hypotheses to the
  ;; person for the threat model.
  :security-reviewer {:family :openai
                      :model "openai/gpt-6-astra"
                      :shape :openai
                      :endpoint "https://openrouter.ai/api/v1"
                      :key-env "OPENROUTER_API_KEY"
                      :params {:reasoning_effort "high"
                               :provider {:only ["openai"] :allow_fallbacks false}}}

  ;; ORCHESTRATOR — gemini-3.8-flash at high effort, over OpenRouter. TRIAGE
  ;; ONLY: one completion with no tools, on a red gate or a note, routing to
  ;; coder | tester | architect | human with a reason and guidance. The
  ;; seat's family, as in claude.edn, and for the same reason: triage is not
  ;; a verifier, so §05 does not constrain it, and the family that sits with
  ;; the human reads the contract the human wrote.
  ;;
  ;; NOT MEASURED. No run has used this seat. High rather than the medium
  ;; this file measures elsewhere, because a triage call is one completion
  ;; over a few thousand tokens with no tool loop — the empty-final-message
  ;; failure this file records for high on a flash tier came from a
  ;; 24-iteration Reviewer dispatch, which this is not. If the first agy-ide
  ;; run shows an empty verdict, the fallback routes mechanically and the
  ;; record says so; drop to medium then.
  ;;
  ;; IT IS THE CODER'S MODEL, and claude.edn moved away from that on
  ;; 2026-10-02: triage judges the Coder's code against the Tester's test, and
  ;; the Coder's own model in that seat is the one kinship the role should
  ;; not have (the family is convention; the model is the bias). Left as it is
  ;; here, said plainly, because no run has used this seat and a model chosen
  ;; for it unmeasured would be a guess dressed as a selection. The first
  ;; agy-ide run decides it, the way the first real project decided the other.
  :orchestrator {:family :google
                 :model "google/gemini-3.8-flash"
                 :shape :openai
                 :endpoint "https://openrouter.ai/api/v1"
                 :key-env "OPENROUTER_API_KEY"
                 :params {:reasoning_effort "high"
                          :provider {:only ["google-vertex"] :allow_fallbacks false}}}}}
